<problem><title>Video review questions</title>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Complete the sentence:</p>
<p>The derivative of <m>f(x) = a^x</m>, where <m>a</m> is a constant, is</p>
  <answer name="v1Review1a">
    <choice><m>f'(x) = x \cdot a^{x-1}</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1">proportional to the function <m>f</m> itself</choice>
    <choice><m>f'(x) = a^x</m></choice>
  </answer>

  <feedback condition="$_choice1"><p>Oops - You're thinking of the rule for taking the derivative of a <term>power function</term>, 
    <me>\text{power function rule:}\;\;\;f'(x) = a \cdot x^{a - 1}\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;f(x) = x^a</me>
This does <em>not</em> match our given <term>exponential function</term>: <m>f(x) = a^x</m>.</p>
<p>When calculating the derivative, it definitely matters where the independent variable <m>x</m> is located! For an <term>exponential</term> function <m>f(x) = a^x</m>, the variable <m>x</m> is the <em>exponent</em> of the function, while for <term>power functions</term> the variable <m>x</m> is the <em>base</em>. Exponential functions follow a completely different rule for differentiation than power functions.</p></feedback>

  <feedback condition="$_choice3"><p>This <em>would</em> be correct if we are considering the magical function where <m>a = e</m>. If the constant <m>a</m> just so happens to be equal to the number <m>e</m>, then we have the special condition where the proportionality constant <m>\bigg( \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{a^h - 1}{h} \bigg)</m> is equal to 1. In this case, we can say that the derivative of the function <m>f</m> is <em>equal</em> to the function <m>f</m>.</p>
    <p>In general (i.e. for <m>a \neq e</m>) all we know is that <alert>the derivative of the function <m>f</m> is proportional</alert> to a <em>number</em>. The proportionality constant is given by <m>\bigg( \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{a^h - 1}{h} \bigg)</m> and corresponds to the value of the function's derivative at 0:
    <md>
      <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \bigg(\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{a^h - 1}{h} \bigg) \cdot a^x</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp=\bigg(f'(0)\bigg) \cdot a^x</mrow>
    </md>
So, what <em>can</em> we say about the derivative of this function <m>f</m> for <em>any</em> base <m>a</m>?</p></feedback>
</exercise>


<aside><title>Formula - The derivative of an <term>exponential function</term> with base <m>a</m>:</title>
  <md>
  <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \bigg(\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{a^h - 1}{h} \bigg) \cdot a^x\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;f(x) = a^x</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp=\bigg(f'(0)\bigg) \cdot a^x</mrow>
  </md>
</aside>


<aside><title>Formula - The derivative of an <term>exponential function</term> with base <m>e</m>:</title>
  <md>
  <mrow>f'(x) \amp= \bigg(\lim_{h \to 0} \frac{e^h - 1}{h} \bigg) \cdot e^x\;\;\;\text{where}\;\;\;f(x) = e^x</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp= \bigg(f'(0)\bigg) \cdot e^x</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp=(1)\cdot e^x</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp=e^x</mrow>
  </md>
</aside>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Using the graph below, explore the derivative of the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m> near <m>x = 0</m>. </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The derivative <m>f'(x)</m> is represented by the $derivLine.styleDescriptionWithNoun, and can be moved by dragging the $dragPoint.styleDescriptionWithNoun. </li>
  <li>The value of the function <m>f</m> at any point can be found by dragging the $dragPointFunction.styleDescriptionWithNoun.

<number name="x" hide="true" displayDecimals="1">-1</number>
<number name="x2" hide="true" displayDecimals="1">1</number>
  <graph size="medium" name="feedbackGraph" xmin="-4" xmax="4" ymin="-2" ymax="6">
    <function applyStyleToLabel="true" name="f">e^x
      <label><m>f(x) = e^x</m></label>
    </function>
    <derivative name="f_prime" hide="true">$f</derivative>
    <line styleNumber="2" slope="$$f_prime($x)" through="$dragPoint" name="derivLine" />
    <point styleNumber="5" fixed="true">(0,1)</point>
    <point styleNumber="2" name="dragPoint" displayDecimals="1" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="right">($x,$$f_prime($x))
      <label><m>f'($x) = <math displayDecimals="1" simplify="true">$$f_prime($x)</math></m></label>
    </point>
    <point styleNumber="4" name="dragPointFunction" displayDecimals="1" applyStyleToLabel="true" labelPosition="left">($x2,$$f($x2))
      <label><m>f($x2) = <math displayDecimals="1" simplify="true">$$f($x2)</math></m></label>
    </point>
  </graph>
  </li>
  
<li><p>Complete the sentence:</p>
<p>The number <m>e</m> is the unique number where...</p>
  <answer name="v1Review1b">
    <choice credit="1">the derivative <m>f'(x)</m>, of the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, is equal to the value of the function itself</choice>
    <choice>the derivative <m>f'(x)</m>, of the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, is always negative</choice>
    <choice>the derivative <m>f'(x)</m>, of the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, goes to 0 as <m>x</m> approaches infinity</choice>
  </answer>
<hint>
<p>Can you match up the two points on the graph? What does that imply about the value of the derivative?</p>
</hint>
  
<feedback condition="$_choice5">Consider the graph above. What is the behavior of the slope of the tangent line (i.e. the derivative) of the function <m>f</m> for all <m>x</m>? The derivative is represented by the $derivLine.styleDescriptionWithNoun, and can be moved by dragging the $dragPoint.styleDescriptionWithNoun. 
<p>Is the derivative of this function ever negative?</p>
</feedback>
<feedback condition="$_choice6">Consider the graph above. What is the behavior of the slope of the tangent line (i.e. the derivative) of the function <m>f</m> for all <m>x</m>? The derivative is represented by the $derivLine.styleDescriptionWithNoun, and can be moved by dragging the $dragPoint.styleDescriptionWithNoun. 
<p>What is the behavior of the derivative for large <m>x</m>?</p></feedback>
</li>
</ul>
</exercise>

</problem>

